  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 AM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nAn area of vorticity in the mid levels (500-700mb) will slide \nsoutheast from near St Louis into the lower Ohio Valley through \nthis morning. Meanwhile, at the surface a warm front is draped \nacross Missouri with MLCAPE of 1000-2000 j/kg situated just to \nour west. Most of the CAMs suggest convection will develop near \nor south of St Louis by 08-09z and then spread southeast into \nareas along and west of the Mississippi between 10- 15z. While \nthe warm front likely remains to our west, MUCAPE reaches near \n2000 j/kg combined with decent mid level lapse rates around 6.5 \nC and deep layer shear of 20-25 kts may support some large hail \nwith these elevated storms. Overall confidence is low and while \nthe severe threat is rather low this morning, it's non-zero.\n\nDepending on how any possible morning convection and associated \ncloud debris play out will dictate how unstable the environment gets \nthis afternoon and evening. Most CAMs now keep most our area dry \nthrough the afternoon, aside from the FV3. However, if morning \nconvection doesn't materialize or exits the region soon enough, \nwould tend to think at least southeast Missouri has a shot for some \nconvection this afternoon. Also, there is still a decent signal for \nscattered convection sinking south across IL/IN this evening ahead \nof a cold front. The I-64 corridor and EVV Tri state probably have \nthe best shot to see stronger storms with that activity before \ninstability begins to wane further as we approach midnight and \nthereafter. Soundings suggest DCAPE exceeding 1000 j/kg which would \nsupport some damaging wind potential. The front may not clear all of \nwest Kentucky until sometime Thursday afternoon, so additional \nisolated storms may be possible on Thursday mainly across the \nsouthern Pennyrile region.\n\nAfter a brief return of higher humidity (dew points in the low 70s) \ntoday, a drier airmass will return behind the cold front Thursday \nafternoon and continue through the weekend. This will result in \ncooler nights again with lows dropping into the low 60s but warm \nafternoons with highs reaching the upper 80s.\n\nUpper level ridging across the southwest CONUS will expand eastward \ninto the OH/TN valleys Sunday into early next week. Medium range \nguidance has been in decent agreement keeping the ridge primarily in \ntact through at least mid or even late next week. Highs return to \nthe 90s on Sunday, and likely will push the mid 90s heading into \nearly next week. Humidity levels remain low initially, but will \nbegin to creep up some especially by Tuesday. They shouldn't be \nanywhere near what we witnessed during previous heatwaves this \nsummer though due to less evaporational transport from agricultural. \nRegardless, hot ambient temperatures will be present (possibly some \nof the warmest we have experienced this season). The LREF \nprobabilities of MaxTs reaching at least 95 degrees are 50-70% by \nnext Tues-Wed. So heat index readings may still exceed 100 several \ndays next week beginning on Tuesday.",
